(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 38-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 260 mg/dL, normal triglyceride and firm nodules over the extensor tendons of his hands (tendon xanthomas). His brother had a heart attack at 44. Which drug regimen is started first?","choices":["High-intensity statin","Low-intensity statin","Evolocumab alone","Fibrate therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The common regimen in heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia is a high-intensity statin, with ezetimibe and nicotinic acid added if needed and aggressive reduction of other vascular risk factors. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 66-year-old African American man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 25%) remains short of breath with slight exertion despite optimized sacubitril-valsartan, a beta blocker, spironolactone and dapagliflozin. Which addition is recommended?","choices":["Dobutamine continued at home","Isosorbide mononitrate on its own","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Verapamil for additional rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For African American patients with reduced ejection fraction in functional class III or IV, hydralazine with nitrates carries a strong recommendation on top of the core classes. Slight-exertion symptoms put him in functional class III. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How does aortic dissection classically present?","choices":["Episodic headache with sweating and palpitations","Abrupt severe chest or back pain, maximal at onset","Painless progressive dyspnea","Gradually worsening chest pain over several days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and maximal at onset is the discriminating feature: pain that builds is less characteristic than pain that arrives at full intensity immediately. Patients often describe it as tearing. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with hypertensive retinopathy achieves good blood pressure control. What can be expected of his retinal findings?","choices":["The changes will progress regardless of control","The changes are permanent once established","Control can lead to regression of the retinopathy","The changes have no relationship to blood pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so the finding is both a prognostic marker and something that can improve with treatment. Retinopathy predicts long-term stroke risk independently of the pressure level. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has chest pain with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. Which artery is the most likely culprit?","choices":["Posterior interventricular","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Right marginal branch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leads I, aVL, V5 and V6 are the lateral leads, and lateral ST elevation points to the left circumflex artery (or a diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery). — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with resistant hypertension is started on lisinopril. Her creatinine rises markedly within a week. What does this suggest?","choices":["Bilateral renal artery stenosis","Expected drug effect requiring no change in approach","Progression of underlying diabetic nephropathy","Acute interstitial nephritis from the medication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Where perfusion depends on angiotensin-mediated efferent arteriolar tone, blocking it drops the filtration pressure sharply. This is one of the recognized clues to renovascular disease, and it is why the agent is contraindicated in bilateral stenosis. A hemodynamically significant unilateral stenosis does the same. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with heart failure diagnosed three years ago has had four days of worsening orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg swelling. How is this episode classified?","choices":["Acute new-onset heart failure","High-output heart failure","Pre-heart failure, stage B","Acute on chronic heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure present for more than a year is chronic, and a sudden worsening on top of it is acute on chronic heart failure, the last step of the sequence from pre-heart failure through exacerbation. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which dyslipidemia is the most common type seen in clinical practice?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mixed dyslipidemia, elevated triglyceride and low-density lipoprotein cholesterol from genetic predisposition plus medical and environmental factors, is the most common type in clinical practice. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with an inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction suddenly develops complete heart block on the monitor. Which artery is most likely occluded?","choices":["Anterior interventricular artery","Diagonal artery","Left circumflex artery","Right coronary artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The right coronary artery supplies the atrioventricular node, so an occlusion that produces complete heart block points to it. That is why right coronary artery infarcts are the ones most likely to cause dysrhythmias. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome after her heart rate rose by 38 within 10 minutes of standing. Which is the most appropriate first treatment?","choices":["Restrict salt and fluid","Increase water and salt intake","Begin vasopressin","Start midodrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Increasing water and salt intake is the first listed treatment, with fludrocortisone as the drug best suited to the syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which drug is best suited to postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome?","choices":["Fludrocortisone","Midodrine","Droxidopa","Phenylephrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone is best with the tachycardia syndrome; because it causes salt and water retention it is avoided in heart failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is found to have a mid-systolic click at the apex at a sports physical. She feels well. Which is the most appropriate initial test?","choices":["Cardiac catheterization","Echocardiogram","Chest radiograph","Electrocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is diagnosed by echocardiogram, which shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets greater than 5 mm bowing into the left atrium during systole. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a week of fatigue and mild breathlessness. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 250. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Acute heart failure is ruled out","Heart failure is likely","Indeterminate for heart failure","Acute heart failure is likely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Between 100 and 400 the peptide has no useful sensitivity or specificity for heart failure, so the clinical picture and imaging have to settle the question. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis with leg swelling and jugular venous distension. Before any procedure, which medical regimen is appropriate?","choices":["Afterload reduction with hydralazine","Low salt, diuretic, aldosterone antagonist","Nitrates, beta blocker, calcium blocker","Warfarin with a heparin bridge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is managed with a low-salt diet, diuretics and an aldosterone antagonist, with bioprosthetic valve replacement preferred when the valve is replaced. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How does dilated cardiomyopathy differ from hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Thickened muscle that contracts poorly","Normal muscle with leaking valves","Thin, ballooned muscle that contracts poorly","Thickened muscle that relaxes poorly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is decreased muscle with poor contraction and loss of elasticity. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is increased muscle that contracts strongly but relaxes poorly. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 63-year-old with long-standing hypertension has an estimated filtration rate that has fallen over eighteen months and a rising urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio. He also has type 2 diabetes. What should be done next?","choices":["Attribute it all to diabetes and stop antihypertensives","Conclude the albuminuria is a laboratory artifact","Evaluate for alternative renal causes too","Attribute it all to hypertension and intensify control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Not all chronic kidney disease in a hypertensive patient is hypertensive, and diabetes must be evaluated. The relationship is bidirectional, so the presence of both does not establish which came first. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man arrives for his first adult screening visit, having eaten lunch an hour ago. Which lipid test can be done today without fasting?","choices":["Calculated low-density lipoprotein","Triglyceride and total cholesterol","Triglyceride with high-density cholesterol","Total and high-density cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Total and high-density cholesterol need no fasting; their ratio is the most efficient and cost-effective way to predict coronary risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How does inspiration change the pulmonic stenosis murmur?","choices":["It does not change","It gets louder","It turns diastolic","It gets softer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonic stenosis murmur increases with inspiration, which raises venous return to the right heart, and it comes with a split second heart sound. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 9, 57"},{"q":"What is the 3-year mortality of symptomatic aortic stenosis?","choices":["35%","25%","10%","75%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic aortic stenosis has a 3-year mortality of 75%. Once symptoms begin, prognosis is severely worsened, which is why symptoms suggestive of the disease prompt an echocardiogram. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is a beta blocker the wrong first choice for an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart?","choices":["The first drug is a sodium-glucose inhibitor","Diabetes makes any drug unnecessary","Beta blockers are banned in all heart failure","A loop diuretic must be started first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stage A the only strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. The beta blocker is a stage B therapy once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with acute severe aortic regurgitation from endocarditis is waiting for valve surgery that is delayed by several hours. Which is the most appropriate temporary stabilization in the intensive care unit?","choices":["Intravenous diuretics and nitroprusside","A fluid bolus to raise her preload","Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty","Oral hydralazine and discharge home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If surgery for acute severe aortic regurgitation is delayed, temporary stabilization may be attempted in the intensive care unit with intravenous diuretics and a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with severe multivessel coronary disease is scheduled for a coronary artery bypass graft. Which conduit is the first choice to bypass his obstructions?","choices":["The superficial femoral vein","A mammary or radial artery","The popliteal vein","The great saphenous vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bypass grafting anastomoses one or both of the mammary arteries, or a radial artery, to the coronary artery distal to the obstructive lesion. A vein is used only if an artery cannot be used. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 76, 77"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is three days out from a myocardial infarction complicated by acute heart failure, with an ejection fraction of 30%. A colleague proposes an implantable defibrillator before discharge. Which step is premature at this point?","choices":["Referring to cardiac rehabilitation","Optimizing her guideline therapy","Starting a wearable defibrillator","Implanting a defibrillator now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure from infarction, the pathway is a wearable defibrillator, optimized therapy and cardiac rehabilitation, then a repeat echocardiogram; the implantable device follows only if there is no improvement. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's blood pressure is 148/88 in the right arm and 132/80 in the left at his first visit. He has no chest pain and his pulses are symmetric and normal. Which arm should be used at follow-up visits?","choices":["Use the right arm for all subsequent measurements","Average the two readings and record 140/84","Compare arm and leg pressures to exclude coarctation","Image immediately for aortic dissection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both arms are measured at the initial visit precisely to find a difference, and the higher consistent arm becomes the one used for follow-up. Averaging the two would systematically understate his pressure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has his pressure recorded at a well visit, and a new staff member flags it as low by comparing it with an adult's normal range. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Compare it with norms for his age","Start a vasopressor for hypotension","Measure it again in adult units","Order an echocardiogram at once"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood pressure norms are affected by age, so a child's reading is judged against the normal range for his age rather than an adult's. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with heart failure has cool extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and increasing confusion. His urine output has fallen and creatinine has doubled. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Acute kidney injury from low perfusion","Urinary retention from prostatic disease","Hypertensive kidney damage from years","Nephrotic syndrome from proteinuria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure and obtundation are signs of low perfusion, and renal dysfunction with acute kidney injury and falling urine output is part of the same picture. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man is discharged after receiving a mechanical aortic valve. Which medication must he be told he will take for life?","choices":["Aspirin","Nitroprusside","Warfarin","Heparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical valve of titanium and carbon needs lifelong anticoagulation, and warfarin, the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, is recommended for long-term management. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman who had rheumatic fever as a child has breathlessness on exertion. At the apex there is a low-pitched rumbling diastolic murmur that begins with an opening snap (a sharp sound just after the second heart sound), best heard with the bell in the left lateral decubitus position (lying on the left side). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis","Aortic regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rumble means stenosis and diastolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the apex, the opening snap after the second heart sound and the left lateral position mark mitral stenosis. Rheumatic heart disease is its most common cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 38, 34"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has firm yellow nodules over the backs of his hands, elbows and heels. His total cholesterol is 780 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride, and both parents have markedly elevated cholesterol. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presentation in childhood with cutaneous xanthomas on the hands, elbows and heels, total cholesterol of about 400 to above 1000 mg/dL, normal triglyceride and two affected parents fits two mutant alleles. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with a baseline low-density lipoprotein of 205 mg/dL has taken a high-intensity statin reliably for three months. His low-density lipoprotein is now 122 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add gemfibrozil","Stop the statin","Add ezetimibe","Change nothing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In severe primary hypercholesterolemia, if low-density lipoprotein remains 100 or more on a high-intensity statin, adding ezetimibe is reasonable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman reports several brief faints over 6 months with a warm, nauseated prodrome, but she cannot recall what was happening before most of them. Her examination is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Arrange an echocardiogram","Order a tilt-table test","Keep a log of each episode","Start fludrocortisone now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A log of pattern and frequency supports a history-based diagnosis of reflex syncope and helps identify the triggers to avoid. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with high-output heart failure from untreated hyperthyroidism has leg edema and breathlessness. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Start verapamil to slow the heart rate","Start dobutamine to support contractility","Treat the thyroid disease and add a diuretic","Refer for heart transplant evaluation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-output failure is treated by correcting the underlying cause of increased demand, here hyperthyroidism, with a diuretic for congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which pair are both non-hypotensive causes of syncope?","choices":["Seizure and anemia","Arrhythmia and embolism","Fear and pain","Diarrhea and hemorrhage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both appear on the non-hypotensive list with hypoglycemia, toxic overdose, electrolyte imbalance, hypoxia, anxiety and the tachycardia syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman's mother had a myocardial infarction at 70 and her brother had one at 50. She asks which relative's heart attack raises her own coronary risk. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Neither relative's","Both relatives'","Only her mother's","Only her brother's"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Family history counts when a male first-degree relative had sudden death or myocardial infarction before 55, or a female first-degree relative before 65. Her brother at 50 qualifies; her mother at 70 does not. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man remains above goal despite the described sequence: adherence confirmed, diuretic optimized, three agents titrated, spironolactone added and tolerated. What now?","choices":["Stop spironolactone and restart from a single agent","Add a second mineralocorticoid antagonist","Refer to a hypertension specialist","Accept the current pressure as his individual goal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Referral is reserved for truly difficult cases after the full sequence has been worked through rather than being an early substitute for it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from volume loss is lightheaded on standing in the clinic but alert, with a standing pressure of 100/62. Which treatment is inappropriate in this setting?","choices":["Reviewing her medications","Slow position changes","Correcting the volume loss","A norepinephrine infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction asks why his clinician raises one medicine at a time rather than starting everything at full strength. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Therapy is optimized as tolerated","Full doses are unsafe in any patient","Only one drug can be taken at once","Medicines stop once symptoms go"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Guideline-directed therapy is prioritized for his comorbidities and optimized as hemodynamically stable, so each class is increased as blood pressure and tolerance allow. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with pulmonary hypertension has pulmonic regurgitation. She has no symptoms of right heart failure. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Treat the underlying condition","Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Lifelong warfarin therapy","Transcatheter valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of pulmonic regurgitation is to treat the underlying condition -- here the pulmonary hypertension, the high-pressure cause -- with referral when it is seen on echo or right heart failure develops. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 64, 61"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man describes episodes of pounding headache, drenching sweats, palpitations and pallor, each lasting about twenty minutes. Between episodes he feels well. His blood pressure is 176/104 during an episode. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Pheochromocytoma","Primary aldosteronism","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The five Ps are pressure, perspiration, palpitations, pallor and tremor, and the paroxysmal pattern with well intervals is what distinguishes it from sustained hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with right ventricular infarction (ST elevation in V4R) is given sublingual nitroglycerin in triage, and her blood pressure falls to 70/40 mmHg. What explains the fall?","choices":["Ventricular aneurysm","Myocardial rupture","Lost preload","Thrombolytic bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitroglycerin reduces preload, which removes the Frank-Starling effect the injured right ventricle depends on. Output drops further, the right ventricle fails and the patient goes into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with cardiomyopathy has had pressures around 92/58 for many months. She has intermittent lightheadedness that comes and goes. How is her hypotension best classified?","choices":["Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Acute reflex hypotension","Acute toxic hypotension","Chronic cardiogenic hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic causes such as cardiomyopathy and reduced ejection fraction heart failure produce chronic hypotension, which waxes and wanes and is intermittently symptomatic. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with non-ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 30% after six months of optimized guideline-directed therapy. She is breathless with exertion but not at rest and is otherwise healthy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Inotropes in the intensive care unit","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","A calcium channel blocker for rate","Heart transplant listing right away"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She meets every criterion: non-ischemic cardiomyopathy with ejection fraction 35% or less, class II, optimal guideline-directed therapy and life expectancy of at least one year. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has anginal chest pain with ST changes, and her physician suspects microvascular angina. Which test is required before that diagnosis can be made?","choices":["Transthoracic echocardiogram","Exercise treadmill test","Coronary calcium score","Coronary angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is a diagnosis of exclusion, and it requires angiography to rule out coronary artery disease; the arteries must be shown to be normal before the label applies. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man had a heart attack last year. He stopped his high-intensity statin because of muscle aches, which then resolved. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Fish oil in place of statins","Moderate-intensity statin","A fibrate instead","Stop all lipid therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If a high-intensity statin is not tolerated in clinical atherosclerotic disease, a moderate-intensity statin is used, so he still receives secondary prevention. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with Parkinson disease reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within minutes of standing and fainted once in his kitchen. He is well hydrated. Supine he is 138/80 with a heart rate of 68; standing he is 106/70 with a heart rate of 70. What is the most likely cause of his syncope?","choices":["A ventricular arrhythmia","Autonomic failure from Parkinson disease","Volume depletion from poor intake","A vasovagal reflex on standing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Parkinson disease is an example cause of neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and a large pressure fall with almost no heart rate rise marks autonomic failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with hypertension was switched to a beta blocker three months ago. His triglyceride has risen from 140 to 260 mg/dL, and his thyroid and kidney tests are normal. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["The rise must be familial disease","The rise means he has hypothyroidism","The beta blocker lowers triglyceride","The beta blocker can raise triglyceride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers can elevate triglyceride, as can estrogen, glucocorticoids, isotretinoin and bexarotene, so a new rise after starting one should be recognized as a secondary cause. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has palpitations and fatigue. There is a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex that radiates to the axilla and is loudest in the left lateral decubitus position (lying on the left side). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blowing means regurgitation and holosystolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the apex, the radiation to the axilla and the left lateral position mark mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 48, 73"},{"q":"When do vasospastic angina episodes most often occur?","choices":["Late afternoon","During exercise","Overnight, before dawn","Right after lunch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with vasospastic angina have pain from midnight to early morning, more often between midnight and 8 in the morning, at rest, coming on and off gradually over 5 to 15 minutes. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 89, 90"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has progressive leg edema and jugular venous distension (distended neck veins). At the apex there is a low-pitched diastolic rumble with an opening snap. Which is the most likely cause of her right-sided heart failure?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Mitral valve stenosis","Aortic valve stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex with an opening snap is mitral stenosis, one of the three causes of right-sided failure with chronic left-sided failure and pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man reports fainting twice after standing. Before any test is ordered, which part of his evaluation is most important for identifying the source?","choices":["A detailed history","A chest radiograph","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of accurately diagnosing the source of orthostatic syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 120 mg/dL and a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 3%. She has no risk-enhancing factors. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Emphasize lifestyle","Moderate-intensity statin","Ezetimibe alone","High-intensity statin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low risk (under 5%) calls for a risk discussion that emphasizes lifestyle to reduce risk factors, rather than a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"How does hypertension prevalence change with age?","choices":["It falls after middle age","It peaks in early adulthood","It remains flat across adult life","It rises sharply"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why advancing age sits among the major risk factors and why a young patient with hypertension raises more suspicion of a secondary cause than an older one does. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with mildly reduced ejection fraction (fraction 46%) is euvolemic on a diuretic. Which drug class is the next best-supported addition?","choices":["Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Evidence-based beta blocker at once","Diltiazem for rate and pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After diuretics, the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor carries a moderate recommendation in mildly reduced ejection fraction, ahead of the weak recommendations for the beta blocker, antagonist and renin-angiotensin drugs. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with new heart failure also describes pressure-like chest pain for the past day. His electrocardiogram shows new T-wave inversions. Which additional laboratory test is indicated?","choices":["Hepatic function panel","Repeat natriuretic peptide","Thyroid hormone level","Cardiac troponin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is ordered when there is concern for an acute infarction or for heart failure secondary to ischemic cardiomyopathy, and chest pain with new inversions raises exactly that concern. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a holosystolic murmur, and the examiner is unsure whether it is tricuspid or mitral in origin. Which bedside maneuver best separates the two?","choices":["Standing up quickly","Full expiration","Sustained handgrip","Deep inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart, so the tricuspid regurgitation murmur grows louder (the Carvallo sign) while mitral regurgitation does not. That distinguishes them. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 71, 9"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a normal echocardiogram asks whether she should take metoprolol, as her father did, to prevent heart failure. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["A loop diuretic is the preventive drug","Dapagliflozin comes first, not a beta blocker","No drug helps until symptoms appear","Metoprolol is the first drug for prevention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart she is stage A, where the first recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor such as dapagliflozin or empagliflozin. A beta blocker enters at stage B. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with an hour of chest pressure at rest has ST depression in two consecutive leads and a standard troponin I of 0.3 ng/mL. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Unstable angina","Stable angina","An ST-elevation myocardial infarction","A non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ST depression greater than 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads with an elevated troponin is a non-ST-elevation infarction. A standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal, and 0.3 ng/mL is well above it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"Which drug class improves survival after a myocardial infarction that leaves reduced left ventricular function?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Beta blockers","Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers improve survival after infarction when left ventricular function is reduced; with preserved function that benefit is not established. Calcium channel blockers do not improve prognosis. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with confirmed resistant hypertension has had his diuretic optimized and his other two agents titrated to maximally tolerated doses. His filtration rate and potassium are normal. Which fourth agent has the most evidence?","choices":["Spironolactone","A second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A beta blocker","Hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, provided the filtration rate and potassium allow. It is added after the foundational three are optimized rather than instead of doing so. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which is a function of apolipoproteins?","choices":["Excretion of cholesterol into the bile","Forming the particle's hydrophobic core","Storage of triglyceride in fat cells","Ligands for cell-surface receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apolipoproteins provide the structural foundation of lipoproteins, activate enzymes important in lipoprotein metabolism and act as ligands for cell-surface receptors. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
